it seems like a pretty consistent  positive benefit if you are trying to  enhance satiety to a meal it's just  slowing down a little bit  other tips i usually give people  aside from eating more slowly would be  avoiding really hyper palatable meals  while you're dieting structuring your  meals with plenty of protein plenty of  fiber plenty of water content and when  you do that when you have high protein  high fiber high water content you're  usually going to find that the energy  density of the meal tends to be pretty  low  and then just including plenty of  unprocessed or minimally processed foods  which again if you're including a lot of  unprocessed or minimally processed foods  you're more likely to include plenty of  foods with lower energy density and with  harder texture  and that's not a hard and fast rule you  can find exceptions to that but if you  are constructing a diet that has a lot  of unprocessed food uh or minimally  processed um and what you're going to  find is a lot of items in there with low  energy density and harder texture so  you're going to be chewing more it's  going to be more bites per meal it's  going to slow down the rate of eating so  there are a lot of things we can focus  on if we're trying to increase satiety  those are the ones that i focus on when  it comes to just what you're putting on  your plate  but one other thing i encourage people  to consider  is adopting more of an acceptance-based  approach to hunger so if you're  embarking on a weight loss diet  or you're trying to maintain a reduced  body weight  you kind of know going into that that  hunger might be an issue it might be  something that you are going to  experience  and  you know have to work through and so  there's really two ways to approach that  if you want to kind of dichotomize it  one would be i am waging war on hunger  and i need to fight this every step of  the way  it is a rejection of hunger it is kind  of classifying and internalizing hunger  as being an objectively negative thing  that you must fight against  and hopefully overcome and kind of  defeat  but if you adopt a more acceptance-based  approach  it's just a shift in mindset uh and  there's some interesting research  showing this to be a pretty effective  strategy when it comes to  experiencing hunger in the context of  dieting  but the idea is that you are accepting  hunger as part of the process now it  doesn't mean you're going to  be excited about it and enjoy hunger  when it's happening but it's a  completely different mindset where  you're focused on  uh acknowledging that hunger  experiencing it not forcing it to go  away not you know waging war on that  hunger but just kind of accepting that  as part of the process and trying to  reframe that and  if you dig into it and you take this  acceptance focused approach to hunger uh  you can find yourself almost looking for  a bit of a silver lining there right so  if you are dieting and you notice some  hunger the first thing you can recognize  is okay this is present and i know that  it's not permanent you know it's going  to come and go when i eat and fast and  you know  periods between meals it'll come and go  so it's not permanent it's not going to  be here forever it's not something that  necessarily requires immediate action  and it also could potentially be  indicative of the fact that i'm  generally on the right track here you  know i'm in an energy deficit and hunger  is one clue  that here i am you know eating a lower  calorie intake which is compatible with  my goal whether it's weight loss or  maintenance of a reduced body weight uh  so i it's it's not that i'm saying  you know the initial question was about  you know fat and satiety it's not that  i'm saying you know there's nothing we  can do about satiety because i think  there are a lot of things when it comes  to satiety and hunger that we can focus  on which can be quite helpful whether it  comes to how we construct a meal or how  we view hunger and satiety in general  but a lot of times the question does end  up being  fat versus carbs go or is protein  actually satiating and i think those are  i understand where the questions come  from but i think if we dig deeper we can  ask more nuanced questions  and when we do that we get answers that  are very practical very actionable and a  lot more helpful than just focusing on  macronutrient breakdown
 

